   "body": "**Adversarial review (Fable) — APPROVE at 5e3ae94**\n\nReviewed from the diff, the test file and the two doc files of `dev-swift` (53170bb) and `origin/docs/ledger-2026-11-09` (2aa40b7), not from the author's conclusions.\n\n**Scope.** One commit on top of `dev-swift`, one file, 9 insertions: `import re` plus the two filters in `RealBodiesTest` that drop the `| 604 |` / `| 607 |` changelog rows and the `| GAP-596 |` row from the temporary copies before the fold. Nothing else.\n\n**Tests run** (`python3 -I -m unittest discover scripts/tests`, 127 tests):\n- pr679 as is, `GITHUB_ACTIONS` unset and `=true`: OK.\n- pr679 with `docs/CHANGELOG-LEGACY.md` + `docs/migration/GAP-LEGACY-VS-SWIFT.md` taken from `origin/docs/ledger-2026-11-09`, unset and `=true`: OK.\n- Control: the `dev-swift` version of the test on those ledger files fails with `[604] != [604, 607]`, as the PR says.\n\n**The drop is needed, not just defensive.** On the ledger branch the steward corrected #607's entries (2aa40b7), so its changelog row and the `GAP-596` row differ from the body in `ledger_real_bodies.json`. Without the drop, `plan()` would hit `changelog #607 is already a row or a fragment with other text` (fatal for that PR), not the benign \"already folded\". `#` of a ledger changelog row is the PR number (`ledger.py` `plan()`, `str(n)`), so the regex targets exactly PR 604/607's rows.\n\n**Regexes against the real files.** `re.match(r\"\\| (604|607) \\|\", …)` is anchored at line start; it matches 0 lines on `dev-swift` and exactly lines 108–109 on the ledger branch (the legacy rows are numbered 1–96, ledger rows carry PR numbers, the tally tables start with words). `startswith(\"| GAP-596 |\")` matches 0 / 1 line. No unrelated row can be dropped; the `| b | …, 596` tally lists are left alone and `fold.check` still passes after the refold.\n\n**The test still asserts something.** With #607's `gap-new` broken in a copy of the fixture (`Section:` → `Sektion:`), `RealBodiesTest` fails `[604] != [604, 607]` on both the `dev-swift` files and the ledger files. Dropping `GAP-596` also makes `assertIn(\"| GAP-596 |\", gap)` meaningful again on a post-batch checkout (it was vacuous there before).\n\n**CI on 5e3ae94.** Fixture tooling tests, Docs consistency, SwiftLint, Pre-commit, gitleaks, CodeQL (actions): success; Swift/app jobs correctly skipped. HFRKit tests (Linux) and CodeRabbit were still in progress when I looked.\n\n**Non-blocking note.** The comment says the copy becomes \"the pre-batch state whatever the checkout\": on a post-batch checkout it keeps #590/#622/#623's rows and the other folded edits. The test does not depend on them (#590 is skipped as malformed, never refolded), so no change needed; \"the state these three PRs expect\" would be more exact.\n\nVerdict: APPROVE. `Direct-PR:` reason present, `direct-pr` label set.\n\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
